QUALITY GATE — HARD REJECTION

After hard safety, schedule, equipment, injury, and blacklist checks pass, evaluate whether the plan is a scientifically good program for the user's selected:

goal: strength, hypertrophy, endurance, or weight loss
experience level: beginner, intermediate, or advanced
days per week
session duration
focus muscles
available equipment
injuries/limitations

Reject the plan if it is technically valid but poor training.

Goal-specific quality rules:

STRENGTH
Reject if:

main strength lifts are not placed early in the session;
loads/reps are not strength-biased;
progression is unclear or unrealistic;
too much fatigue/junk volume interferes with performance;
focus muscles/lifts are not prioritized.

HYPERTROPHY
Reject if:

weekly hard-set volume is too low for focus muscles;
major non-focus muscles are severely undertrained;
one muscle receives excessive volume at the expense of balance/recovery;
exercise choices are not trackable for progressive overload;
volume is padded with junk work instead of useful hard sets.

ENDURANCE
Reject if:

the plan lacks sufficient conditioning or muscular-endurance work;
reps/rests are not endurance-biased;
sessions are mostly hypertrophy/strength work mislabeled as endurance;
lower-impact options are not used when injury requires them.

WEIGHT LOSS
Reject if:

the plan lacks adequate calorie-expenditure support through conditioning, circuits, carries, or higher-density work;
resistance training is too low to preserve muscle;
injury-safe low-impact conditioning is ignored;
the plan is just a hypertrophy plan renamed as weight loss.

Experience-level quality rules:

BEGINNER
Reject if:

exercise selection is too complex;
volume is excessive;
progression is too aggressive;
too many advanced variations are used;
injury work is unclear or unsafe.

INTERMEDIATE
Reject if:

volume/frequency/progression are not meaningfully structured;
focus muscles are not prioritized;
major movement patterns are missing without reason;
fatigue is poorly distributed.

ADVANCED
Reject if:

volume, intensity, progression, or specialization are too generic;
focus muscles do not receive advanced-level prioritization;
fatigue management is not explicit;
the plan lacks enough stimulus to justify advanced programming.

Injury-quality rule:
Any injury must change exercise selection, loading, range of motion, impact level, or progression as needed. Reject if the plan merely adds caution notes while keeping clearly unsuitable exercises.

Focus-muscle rule:
The selected focus muscles must receive clear priority through at least one of:

higher weekly direct volume,
earlier placement in sessions,
higher frequency,
better progression detail,
more recoverable fatigue distribution.

Movement-quality rule:
Reject if the week lacks basic movement balance without a valid equipment/injury reason:

squat/knee-dominant pattern,
hinge/hip-dominant pattern,
horizontal push,
horizontal pull,
vertical push if feasible,
vertical pull if feasible,
core/carry/stability,
relevant rehab/prehab.